  "analysis": {
    "one_liner": "Tata Capital is the Tata Group's NBFC, lending to retail, SME and corporate customers plus a vehicle-finance and housing-finance book, under the Tata Sons umbrella.",
    "tags": [
      "Listed Oct 2025",
      "AUM crossed \u20b93 lakh cr",
      "ROE ~14%",
      "Promoter 85.4%",
      "17% off August peak"
    ],
    "whats_happening": "Q2 FY27 (quarter to Sept 2026) results came out on 27 October 2026: consolidated PAT of roughly \u20b91,660 crore, up about 51% year-on-year, and total AUM crossing \u20b93 lakh crore, up roughly 26% year-on-year. The stock rose about 5% on the results day. Separately, RBI's Governor said in late October 2026 that parent Tata Sons, if it stays on the Upper-Layer NBFC list, would be required to list separately - an unresolved overhang for the group, not for Tata Capital itself, since Tata Capital already listed in October 2025.",
    "plain_terms": "This is a lending business - it borrows money cheaply (helped by the Tata name and AAA ratings) and lends it out at a higher rate across personal loans, SME loans, corporate loans, vehicle loans and housing loans. The loan book and profit are both growing at a healthy clip and bad loans are falling, which is good. The tension: it's a very recently listed stock (just over a year of trading history), its profitability (return on equity, ~14%) is still well below an established peer like Bajaj Finance (~20%), and one part of its book - vehicle/motor finance - is being deliberately shrunk because of past bad-loan problems there. Reliable valuation numbers (P/E, price-to-book) could not be pinned down from available sources, so whether the stock is cheap or expensive relative to that growth is genuinely unclear right now.",
    "quarters": "Q1 FY27 (June quarter): consolidated PAT around \u20b91,547-1,628 crore (sources vary slightly), up ~56% YoY; AUM \u20b92.9 lakh crore, up 22% YoY. Q2 FY27 (September quarter, reported 27 Oct 2026): NII \u20b93,696 crore (+23% YoY), PAT around \u20b91,660 crore (+51% YoY) - one outlet reported \u20b91,832 crore/+64%, but five independent sources corroborate the \u20b91,660 crore figure, so that is the more reliable number. AUM \u20b93,08,030 crore, +26% YoY, +6% quarter-on-quarter. Cost-to-income improved 438 bps YoY to 35.3%, and gross/net NPA improved to 1.9%/0.8% (consolidated) from 2.2%/1.1% a year earlier. Management kept FY27 AUM growth guidance at 23-25%, said festive retail/vehicle demand was buoyant, but is deliberately prioritising profitability over volume in motor finance - that book is shrinking and growth there isn't expected to resume before Q4 FY27.",
    "performance_read": "There is no real long-term track record here - the stock listed only in October 2025, just over a year ago, so claims about multi-year compounding or drawdowns cannot be supported yet. Within that short window: the stock is down 16.4% from its 6 August 2026 peak of \u20b9382.7, trading near \u20b9319.9, with a 52-week range of \u20b9297.3-\u20b9382.7. Since listing, the stock is down about 3% in absolute terms, but that is actually better than the Nifty (-8.6%) and peer Bajaj Finance (-8.9%) over the same period - so on a relative basis it has held up better than the market even while falling. There have been no splits, demergers or other corporate actions to adjust for.",
    "valuation_read": "Not verified - check on Screener. Different sources quoted P/E anywhere from roughly 23x to 44x and price-to-book anywhere from roughly 2.8x to 4.2x, with no reliable consensus found, so none of those numbers should be treated as settled. Qualitatively: the business commands a growth-stock-type rating (one brokerage frames a fair value around 3.4x FY28 estimated book) despite an ROE (~14%) well below Bajaj Finance's (~20.6%), which would mean the market is paying up for the Tata parentage, AAA credit ratings and expected AUM growth rather than for current profitability. Whether that premium is justified or already fully priced in cannot be judged without a verified current multiple.",
    "growth_read": "Growth here is both volume- and margin-led, which is the healthier combination: AUM grew 26% YoY to over \u20b93 lakh crore (32% YoY excluding motor finance), while margins also improved - cost-to-income fell 438 bps YoY and credit costs fell to 0.9% annualised from 1.3% a year ago. The one exception is motor finance, where management is explicitly choosing to shrink the book to fix profitability rather than chase volume, with growth there not expected to return until Q4 FY27.",
    "health_read": "As an NBFC, the relevant health check is asset quality and capital, not operating cash flow. On that front the picture is improving: consolidated gross NPA fell to 1.9% and net NPA to 0.8% (from 2.2%/1.1% a year ago), provision coverage improved to 59%, and capital adequacy stands at 17.3% (Tier-I 14.7%), comfortably above the 15% regulatory minimum. Leverage (debt-to-equity around 5.5x) is high but normal for an NBFC of this scale and is backed by AAA ratings. The main residual risk is business risk - specifically the legacy motor-finance NPA problem being worked through - rather than financial/solvency risk, given the comfortable capital cushion.",
    "ownership_read": "Promoters - Tata Sons (78.8%), TMF Holdings (4.4%) and Tata Investment Corporation (1.95%) - together hold 85.41% of the company, unchanged across the last three reported quarters, with no pledging of promoter shares identified in available sources. FIIs hold about 5.1% and DIIs about 3.9%. No specific marquee outside investor (sovereign wealth fund, large global fund, etc.) could be identified on the register from available sources - if you were expecting a well-known name here, it does not appear to be present based on what could be verified.",
    "strengths": [
      "AUM crossed \u20b93,08,030 crore in Q2 FY27, up 26% YoY, with the ex-motor-finance book growing faster at ~32% YoY",
      "Consolidated gross and net NPA improved to 1.9% and 0.8% respectively, down from 2.2%/1.1% a year earlier",
      "Capital adequacy ratio of 17.3% (Tier-I 14.7%) is comfortably above the regulatory 15% minimum",
      "Cost-to-income ratio improved 438 bps YoY to 35.3%, alongside AAA/A1+ credit ratings from CRISIL and ICRA"
    ],
    "watchpoints": [
      "Motor finance book is being deliberately shrunk due to elevated legacy NPAs, with growth there not expected to resume before Q4 FY27",
      "ROE of roughly 14% remains well below established peer Bajaj Finance's ~20.6%, despite a premium growth-stock style rating",
      "P/E and price-to-book figures conflict sharply across sources (P/E 23x-44x, P/B 2.8x-4.2x) - not reliably verified, so valuation comfort cannot be confirmed either way",
      "Parent Tata Sons remains on RBI's Upper-Layer NBFC list, with RBI's governor indicating in late October 2026 that this could eventually require Tata Sons itself to list - an unresolved group-level regulatory overhang"
    ],
    "one_thing": "Whether the motor-finance book stabilises and resumes growth from Q4 FY27 as management has guided, and whether ROE closes the gap toward Bajaj Finance's level - that will show whether this is a maturing, de-risked lender or still working through its weakest segment.",
    "data_confidence": "Moderate",
    "data_notes": "Q2 FY27 revenue, AUM, NII and asset-quality figures were cross-checked across multiple independent sources (company investor presentation, Business Standard, Kotak Neo, GuruFocus, MarketScreener) and are consistent; one outlier PAT figure (\u20b91,832 crore vs the corroborated \u20b91,660 crore) was set aside. Price and return figures are from the verified market data supplied. P/E, price-to-book and book-value-per-share could not be reconciled across sources and should be checked directly on Screener before use. ROA/ROCE and specific IPO anchor investor names could not be verified. Because the stock has traded for just over a year, no long-horizon (multi-year) performance history exists yet."
